# Cross-Platform Development (PC & Android)

This architecture is designed for **PC and Android** deployment. All code must work on both platforms.

## Platform-Agnostic Principles
1. Write code that works identically on PC and Android by default
2. Use Unity's cross-platform APIs (they handle platform differences internally)
3. ONLY use preprocessor directives when absolutely necessary
4. Test mentally: "Will this work on both platforms?"

## File Access Rules

| Use Case | API | Notes |
|----------|-----|-------|
| Load assets at runtime | `Resources.Load<T>()` | Works on PC & Android |
| Save/Load user data | `Application.persistentDataPath` | Unity handles path per platform |
| Read-only bundled files | `Resources/` folder | NOT StreamingAssets |
| PlayerPrefs | `PlayerPrefs` | Works identically on both |

## AVOID on Cross-Platform
- `System.IO.File` for reading bundled assets (fails on Android APK)
- `Application.streamingAssetsPath` with `File.ReadAllText` (Android requires UnityWebRequest)
- Hardcoded file paths with backslashes
- Platform-specific APIs without preprocessor guards

## When Preprocessor Directives ARE Needed
Use ONLY for truly platform-specific features:

```csharp
#region Platform Specific

#if UNITY_ANDROID && !UNITY_EDITOR
private void HandleAndroidBackButton()
{
    if (Input.GetKeyDown(KeyCode.Escape))
        ShowExitDialog();
}
#endif

#if UNITY_STANDALONE
private void HandleWindowResize()
{
    Screen.SetResolution(width, height, fullscreen);
}
#endif

#endregion
```

## Input Considerations
- Design for both touch (Android) and mouse/keyboard (PC)
- Button sizes: minimum 48x48 pixels for touch targets
- Consider UI scaling for different screen densities

## Performance Considerations
- Android: be mindful of memory, battery, thermal throttling
- Use object pooling for frequently spawned objects
- Optimize draw calls (batching, atlases)

## ALWAYS
1. Use `Resources.Load<T>()` for runtime asset loading
2. Use `Application.persistentDataPath` for save files
3. Use `JsonUtility` for serialization
4. Write platform-agnostic code by default

## NEVER
1. Use `File.ReadAllText` on StreamingAssets (breaks Android)
2. Use preprocessor directives unless absolutely platform-specific
3. Hardcode file paths with backslashes
4. Assume desktop-only features work on mobile
